Phase 6b: a refused key breaks the connection, once

A 401 or a 403 is not a transient fault and retrying it is pointless. The
connection is marked broken, the count is left exactly as it was, and the
timeline gets one connection_lost line saying why the number stopped moving.
A frozen count with no explanation is indistinguishable from a racer who
stopped selling.

Frozen by writing nothing at all. Re-writing the same value would still make
the number this job's decision rather than the last one the provider
confirmed, and the assert is on the absence of the write for that reason.

One event, not one per run: markConnectionBroken is a compare-and-set on
`connection_status = 'connected'`, so two runs failing together produce one
transition and the loser announces nothing. Matching is on the error's class,
never its message — Stripe's own text quotes the key back.

No retry storm, structurally. The connection is read before the provider is
touched, so a broken one costs a single failed call and then nothing on
every later poll. A test asserts the provider is never contacted.

A plain network failure stays `provider_failed` and is retried, which is the
distinction that stops one bad afternoon from disconnecting everybody.

